
@attribute [Route(GlobalValues.Path_Components_Switch)]
<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Page_Title_Components_Switch</PageTitle>

<h1 class="page-heading">@GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Components_Switch Component</h1>

<p>
    The Switch component is a simple toggle component that is either on or off communicated to assistive technologies via the use or the <span class="code-span">role="switch""</span> and the
    <span class="code-span">aria-checked</span> attributed "true" or "false" for the on/off states.
</p>
<p>
    You can use either use the <span>@@bind:SwitchState</span> for two-way binding or the event, <span class="code-span">EventCallbac&lt;bool&gt;</span> with one-way binding.
    By default the switch and label will be together and aligned to the right of the parent container as below. Clicking on either the label or the switch with toggle it its state.
</p>
<p>
    The Switch components has an <span class="code-span">AriaDisabled</span> parameter which when applied will style the control to look visually disabled but still allowing focus with the normal focus indicator. 
    I have added code to prevent the switch from working when its in a aria disabled state.
</p>
<p>
    You can also add the html <span class="code-span">disabled</span> attribute to the Switch tag which will make it appear disabled as well as remove it from the Accessibility Tree
    so it will not be accessible to screen readers.
</p>
<p>
    There is always constant debate around whether controls should ever be disabled with either the html <span class="code-span">disabled</span> attribute or even the <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span>
    attribute. You will need to decide what's best for your use case.
</p>
<p>
    By default the switch will align to the right of its container, with its label to the left. You can force the label to the left hand side of the container whilst the switch remains to the right 
    of its container.
</p>

<h2 id="switch-keyboard" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="switch-keyboard">
    <li><kbd>Space</kbd> - when focus is on the Switch, toggles the state of the switch.</li>
    <li><kbd>Enter</kbd> - when focus is on the Switch, toggles the state of the switch.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    The following three switches show the different states and alignments. The first switch is the default alignment and is enabled. The next two switches are in a disabled state, the first of
    these using the <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span> attribute set by the  <span class="code-span">AriaDisabled</span> parameter, as such it can still receive focus. The last
    switch has the html <span class="code-span">disabled</span> attribute so it is removed from the Accessibility Tree and will not receive focus.
</p>

<section class="section-box" style="max-width:320px">

    <Switch @bind-SwitchState="@_airplaneSwitchState" Label="Airplane mode:" AriaDisabled="false" SpaceBetween="false" style="margin-block:1rem;" />
    <Switch @bind-SwitchState="@_bluetoothSwitchState" Label="Bluetooth:" AriaDisabled="true" SpaceBetween="true" style="margin-block:1rem;" />
    <Switch @bind-SwitchState="@_wifiSwitchState" Label="WiFi:" AriaDisabled="false" SpaceBetween="true" style="margin-block:1rem;" disabled />
</section>

<h3 id="screen-reader-quirks" class="@GlobalValues.Info_Box_Heading_Class" style="margin-top:var(--br-unit-space-9);">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h3>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li><b>Narrator</b> paired with <b>Firefox</b> announces button on/off instead of switch on/off.</li>
</ul>

@code{
    public bool _airplaneSwitchState = false;
    public bool _wifiSwitchState = true;
    public bool _bluetoothSwitchState = false;
}

<section style="margin-top:var(--br-unit-space-9);">
    <h2 class="page-heading">Installation</h2>

    <p>
        <strong>Note: </strong> It is a requirement that the Blazor Ramp Core <span class="code-span">core-live-region.js</span> script, <span class="code-span">LiveRegionService</span>, and
        associated <span class="code-span">&lt;AnnouncementHistory /&gt;</span> component are added alongside this component’s specific
        requirements (a stylesheet reference in this instance). You do not need to add the BlazorRamp.Core package separately, as BlazorRamp.Core is included as a transitive package, you only need to add the packages that contain the components
        that you wish to use i.e the <b>BlazorRamp.Switch</b> NuGet package.
    </p>
    <p>
        Please follow the instructions in the <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Class" href="@GlobalValues.Path_Overview_Getting_Started">@GlobalValues.Main_Nav_Section_Heading_About - @GlobalValues.Page_Heading_Overview_Getting_Started</a>
        ensuring that in the <span class="code-span">&lt;head&gt;</span> section you also add the Switch stylesheet as follows:
    </p>
    <CodeBox CodeBlock="@CodeSnippets.Add_Switch_Style_Sheet" AriaLabel="Add switch stylesheet code block" />

</section>

<section class="code-table">

    <h2 id="switch-component-parameters" class="code-table__heading">Switch component parameters</h2>

    <table aria-labelledby="switch-component-parameters">

        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th scope="col">Parameter</th>
                <th scope="col">Type</th>
                <th scope="col">Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Label</td>
                <td>string</td>
                <td>The text displayed to the left of the switch.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>SwitchState</td>
                <td>bool</td>
                <td>Indicates the state of the switch on/off (true/false).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>AriaDisabled</td>
                <td>bool</td>
                <td>
                    Indicates if the switch is in a disabled state using the <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span> attribute.
                    <strong>Note: </strong> Both this attribute and the disabled attribute whill have the same styling applied but with <span class="code-span">aria-disabled</span>
                    the switch will still receive focus with the normal focus indicator intact.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>SpaceBetween</td>
                <td>bool</td>
                <td>
                    The switch is a block element and will take the width of its container, and b default the label will sit next to the switch.
                    Setting SpaceBetween to true will force the label and switch to opposite ends of its container.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>SwitchStateChanged</td>
                <td>EventCallbac&lt;bool&gt;</td>
                <td>
                    An event raised when the switch is toggled indicating its current state.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>AdditionalAttributes</td>
                <td>Dictionary&lt;string, object&gt;?</td>
                <td>Sets additional attributes applied to the component's root element.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</section>

